### Step 2: Edit KVM (VMX) to Add Exit Counters & Periodic Prints

#### a) Modify the Intel VMX path

**File:** `arch/x86/kvm/vmx/vmx.c`

Add the counters and helpers near the top:

```c
#include <linux/atomic.h>
#define KVM_VMX_MAX_EXIT_REASONS 128
#define KVM_VMX_PRINT_INTERVAL 10000 // prints every 10k total exits

static atomic64_t kvm_vmx_exit_total = ATOMIC64_INIT(0);
static atomic64_t kvm_vmx_exit_counts[KVM_VMX_MAX_EXIT_REASONS];

static const char *vmx_exit_reason_name(unsigned int r) { 
    /* switch with names */ 
}

static void kvm_vmx_maybe_dump_counts(void) { 
    /* printk header + per-exit lines */ 
}
```

Hook in at the start of the VM-exit handler:

```c
static int __vmx_handle_exit(struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu, fastpath_t exit_fastpath)
{
    union vmx_exit_reason exit_reason = vmx_get_exit_reason(vcpu);
    u32 basic = exit_reason.basic;
    
    if (basic < KVM_VMX_MAX_EXIT_REASONS)
        atomic64_inc(&kvm_vmx_exit_counts[basic]);
    atomic64_inc(&kvm_vmx_exit_total);
    kvm_vmx_maybe_dump_counts();
    
    /* ... rest of handler ... */
}
```

*Figure 5: Insertion point inside `__vmx_handle_exit()` (top of handler)*

![VMX Handler Modification](images/image7.png)

![Code Implementation](images/image8.png)

Optional beacon on module init (helps verify module load):

```c
int __init vmx_init(void)
{
    pr_info("KVM/VMX: stats patch loaded (vmx_init beacon)\n");
    /* ... */
}
```

#### b) Rebuild and install only the KVM modules for this running kernel

```bash
make M=arch/x86/kvm -j"$(nproc)" modules
sudo make M=arch/x86/kvm modules_install INSTALL_MOD_DIR=kernel
sudo depmod -a "$(uname -r)"
sudo cp -v arch/x86/kvm/kvm-intel.ko "$(modinfo -n kvm_intel)"
sudo depmod -a "$(uname -r)"
```

After patching the code in `vmx.c`, git push and then git fetch to O-vm and finally check through grep if we have our source code:

```bash
grep -n "stats patch loaded" ~/linux/arch/x86/kvm/vmx/vmx.c
grep -n "VM-Exit stats" ~/linux/arch/x86/kvm/vmx/vmx.c
```

![Source Code Verification](images/image9.png)

![Grep Results](images/image10.png)

*Figure 6: Successful KVM module build and installation*

![Module Build Success](images/image11.png)

#### c) Reload (or reboot) to ensure the patched module is active

```bash
sudo virsh list --state-running # destroy guest if running
sudo systemctl stop libvirtd virtqemud virtlogd
sudo modprobe -r kvm_intel kvm || true
sudo modprobe kvm
sudo modprobe kvm_intel nested=1
sudo systemctl start libvirtd
# (or simply: sudo reboot)
```

*Figure 7: dmesg showing the vmx_init beacon after reload*

![Module Reload](images/image10.png)

---

### Step 3: Boot Inner VM and Observe printk Output

#### a) Enable nested KVM (if not already)

```bash
echo Y | sudo tee /sys/module/kvm_intel/parameters/nested
```

#### b) Start and attach to the inner VM, then generate exits

```bash
sudo virsh start inner-ubuntu
sudo virsh console inner-ubuntu # login
for i in {1..50000}; do cat /proc/cpuinfo >/dev/null; done
```

![VM Start](images/image13.png)

![Generate Exits](images/image14.png)

#### c) Watch kernel logs for periodic stats (every 10k total exits)

```bash
sudo dmesg -w | grep -E "KVM/VMX: (=====|exit=|vmx_init beacon)"
```

*Figure 8: VM-Exit statistics printing in dmesg at the configured interval*

![Exit Statistics](images/image15.png)

